% concepts.bib — the gcalculus COTYPE: the cross-session shadow ledger, as claim records.
%
% Each entry is BOTH a ledger entry and a claim: it carries an axis-signature, its position
% relative to the original request, and a `check` that RUNS.  That is the unification — the
% ledger's own rules (the over-decode guard, axis totality, guard coverage) stop being questions
% the README asks a reader and become witnesses the gate resolves.
%
% Keys are GRADED (paperkit's Λ·key·graded): `shadow/<axis>/<name>` and `probe/<line>/<name>`, so
% the axis structure is visible to the claim-DAG rather than buried in a field — `rests-on` edges
% and coverage are family-aware, and each is a DISTINCT witness under --without-K.
%
% APPEND-ONLY, RETIREMENT-LOGGED.  A retirement is carried by `rests-on` to the entry that
% SUPERSEDES it, and by the claim prose.  It was briefly carried by a `retired = {...}` field
% instead — which paperkit's parser does not read, so the one retired entry parsed as live and
% the discipline this header declares was enforced through a field the engine drops.  The
% vocabulary is closed (`from`, `rests-on`, `reads`, and the scalars); anything else is dropped
% silently, which is a claim nothing checks.  `probe/every-bib-field-is-read` refuses one now.
%
% An entry is never deleted; it is retired with a RETIRED note
% and STAYS.  probe/monotone/monotone is that rule as a witness.

% ── the shadows: registered moves ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

